s: {red_squares}\")\n    \n    if last_action_result.get('level_completed'):\n        print(\"LEVEL COMPLETED!\")\n        break\n    if red_squares < 2:\n        print(\"Target collected!\")\n        break\n    if not targets:\n        print(\"Target disappeared!\")\n        break\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "Step 46: Player cols 17-20, rows 7-9\n  Targets: 1\n    cols 18-19, rows 4-5\n  Red squares: 2\nStep 47: Player cols 17-20, rows 6-8\n  Targets: 1\n    cols 18-19, rows 4-5\n  Red squares: 2\nStep 48: Player cols 17-20, rows 5-7\n  Targets: 0\n  Red squares: 1\nTarget collected!", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}], "hasExactModelContext": false, "requestIndexWithinTurn": null, "messageCount": 0}, "context": {"sections": [{"label": "USER PROMPT", "content": "The code executed 6 actions in the previous sequence.\nExecuted actions: UP, UP, UP, UP, UP, UP.\nYou are still on the same level.\nCurrent state: step 46, level 1.\nValid actions right now: UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, MOUSE.\nOnly tool: `python`. It receives `current_frame`, `previous_frame`, `history`, `transitions`, `last_transition`, `valid_actions`, `last_action_result`, and `action(actions)`. It also provides `last_animation` (per-action frames of your last `action(...)` call) and `frame_stats` (how much this game has animated so far) -- optional; use them only if you judge the intermediate motion informative rather than incidental.\nOnly letter-coded board views and lightweight metadata are exposed; raw numeric color IDs are not available.\nKeep tool output compact: use `current_frame.segmentation` as the primary view, and `current_frame.ascii` only for a small specific region; never print full boards.\nFor the most recent change, compare `previous_frame` to `current_frame`, or `last_transition.before_frame` to `last_transition.after_frame`; `history[-1].frame` is the current frame, not the previous one.\nUse 